Message-ID: <4AAACE4D.5070901@northwestern.edu>

Hi all,

whether frequency or probability weights are used for estimating ATE 
should not make a difference, though for the variance estimate.

Indeed, Zelig perfectly works when used for a model-based estimation of 
ATE using full-matching and the corresponding matchit() weights:

m.out <- matchit(treat ~ age + educ + black + hispan + nodegree + 
married + re74 + re75,
   data = lalonde, method = 'full', discard = 'both')
z.out <- zelig(re78 ~ treat, data = match.data(m.out), model = 'ls', 
weights = 'weights')
x.out0 <- setx(z.out, treat = 0)
x.out1 <- setx(z.out, treat = 1)
s.out <- sim(z.out, x = x.out0, x1 = x.out1)
summary(s.out)

It seems that Zelig gets problems with the weights (the sim() command) 
when using the more sensitive approach of estimating a separate 
regression model for each group as outlined in my first e-mail.

Thanks for all your thoughts,
Peter

